  "cta": "Subscribe: you pick the next test",
  "next_test": "Hit the top: what breaks?",
  "caption": "How much can ONE Lego brick hold? 🧱 40 kg, 400 kg or 4 tons? We press a single 2x2 brick, like the Open University test. Place your bet before the end 👇 #physics #science #engineering #lego #hydraulicpress #mythbusting #3d",
  "description": [
    "How much weight can one single 2x2 Lego brick hold? We load one brick in a press, step by step, until it gives way.",
    "The numbers come from a test by the Open University's engineering department for the BBC (2012): a standard 2x2 brick was loaded with a hydraulic ram and a load cell. On average it gave way at 4,240 N, about 432 kg. That is the weight of about 375,000 bricks stacked on top of it, a tower about 3.5 km high.",
    "In our rounds the brick holds 100 kg, then 300 kg, then gives way at 432 kg.",
    "Why: the brick is made of hard ABS plastic, and the load is shared by its four thick walls. At the limit the plastic yields: it keeps squashing without the load going up, then gives way. It does not shatter like glass.",
    "The animation is a simulation built on the measured value of this test; the shape of the squashed brick is illustrative."
  ],
  "sources_detail": [
    "Open University (Dr Ian Johnson's team) for BBC News, Dec 2012: average 2x2 brick crushed at 4,240 N under a hydraulic ram with a load cell (Smithsonian, 2012; The Brothers Brick, 2012). 4,240 / 9.81 = 432 kg.",
    "375,000 stacked bricks, tower about 3.5 km (2.17 miles) (BBC via The Brothers Brick, 2012). Plastic failure: the brick kept deforming without the load increasing, until the ABS gave way.",
    "Rounds 1 and 2 (100 kg, 300 kg holds) follow from the 432 kg limit. Simulated: squash shape, stress whitening and timing are illustrative."
  ],
